    <info>
        <language>en-US</language>
        <category>Met</category>
        <event>Tsunami Advisory</event>
        <responseType>Prepare</responseType>
        <urgency>Future</urgency>
        <severity>Moderate</severity>
        <certainty>Possible</certainty>
        <eventCode>
            <valueName>SAME</valueName>
            <value>NWS</value>
        </eventCode>
        <eventCode>
            <valueName>NationalWeatherService</valueName>
            <value>TSY</value>
        </eventCode>
        <effective>2025-07-30T16:20:00-07:00</effective>
        <onset>2025-07-30T16:20:00-07:00</onset>
        <expires>2025-07-30T17:50:00-07:00</expires>
        <senderName>NWS National Tsunami Warning Center</senderName>
        <headline>Tsunami Advisory issued July 30 at 4:20PM PDT by NWS National Tsunami Warning Center</headline>
        <description>If you are located in this coastal area, move off the
beach and out of harbors and marinas.

Tsunami advisories mean that a tsunami capable of producing
strong currents or waves dangerous to persons in or very near
the water is expected or is already occurring.  Areas in the
advisory should not expect widespread inundation.  Tsunamis
are a series of waves dangerous many hours after initial
arrival time.  The first wave may not be the largest.

At 425 PM Pacific Daylight Time on July 29 an earthquake with
preliminary magnitude 8.8 occurred 80 miles southeast of
Petropavlovsk Kamchatka.

The tsunami advisory will remain in effect until further notice.
Refer to the internet site tsunami.gov for more information.
</description>
        <web>http://www.weather.gov</web>
        <parameter>
            <valueName>AWIPSidentifier</valueName>
            <value>TSUWCA</value>
        </parameter>
